Description: hw/usb/hcd-imx21: Reach a device only when the block is set up as hardware needs
 The model completed transfers however the guest had set the block up, so
 a kernel that enumerated here got -110 from every control transfer on a
 real Chumby, with connects still seen. Now, as sources support:
 .
 - With CLK_CTRL's HST and MAIN or PCCR0's USBOTG gates off, nothing runs
   (the stock board file enables each before use).
 - A device is out of reach, its ETDs ending "device not responding",
   unless the USB clock is within USB's 0.25% of 48 MHz, the root port's
   pins are multiplexed to it as the stock board file does, PB25-PB29 are
   outputs as it sets them, and for host 1 USBCTRL's HOST1_TXEN_OE is set,
   as the stock driver does before each transfer. That these are all
   needed, and what the wire does without them, is inferred from those
   sources: the reference manual was not to hand.
 .
 USBCTRL now resets to 0x000f1000 and survives a block reset, as a stock
 driver that only sets TXEN_OE and a running Chumby's 0x000f1002 imply;
 HWMODE's top half reads 0x2020 and ROOTHUBA 0x01000103 as on a Chumby.
 Nothing is required of USBCTRL's other bits, MISCCONTROL or HWMODE's
 transceiver modes: no source says what they do to a transfer.
 .
 A kernel's GPTs and the PWM now count at PERCLK1 as the clock module
 makes it, so a kernel whose clock tree assumes another oscillator keeps
 the wrong time, as on the device. The board's hub can be plugged into a
 root port, with devices given no port going behind it.
